{"title":"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 13","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems (page does not exist)\">1999 CEMC Pascal #13</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Fermat_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1999 CEMC Fermat Problems (page does not exist)\">1999 CEMC Fermat #11</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(answer_choices)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (answer choices)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2.5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2.5</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In <i>Circle Land</i>, the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/f/86f9c22428001d4ccb358bc49f5ccc62bf68d248.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$207$\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25ee762ac51a202f964673520cae42da4e34ac56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4520$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> are shown in the following way:\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>In <i>Circle Land</i>, what number does the following diagram represent?\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/a/52a1d29813e6966350139c1fcd701309df4f3edc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ 30 105 \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ 30 150 \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ 3105 \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ 3015 \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ 315$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"533\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Using the first two provided diagrams, we can see that the rings represent what place a specific number is in, or what. For example, if a number has two rings around it, then that number represents the hundreds digit and would be multiplied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/6/db61fd75a1a8bb6fa2b202b6b73daf9b4c9838d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"15\" />, while a number with five rings around it would represent the hundred thousands digit, and would be multiplied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/d/e7d8b576968ccdbfeff38053b7c606905adb945e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Using this, we can count the number of rings around each number for the number that we are trying to find. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> has four rings around it, so there must be a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the ten thousands place. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> has two rings around it, so there must be a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the hundreds place. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> has no rings around it, so the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> must be in the ones place. The rest of the digits in the number must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>The number that corresponds to this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/d/56dc09b0de9fcf10f2fe99d2bae79f670af6c981.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(A) } 30105}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"94\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28answer_choices.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(answer_choices)\">Solution 2 (answer choices)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (answer choices)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can use the same logic from solution 1. However, we can eliminate answer choices.\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> must be in the ten thousands place, we can eliminate answer choices C, D, and E, leaving only answer choices A and B.\n</p><p>We can now see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> must be in the ones place because it doesn't have any rings around it. This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/d/56dc09b0de9fcf10f2fe99d2bae79f670af6c981.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(A) } 30105}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"94\" height=\"30\" /> must be the answer.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2.5\">Solution 2.5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2.5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Rather than comparing the ones digits of answer choices A and B, we can compare the tens place.\n</p><p>In the diagram, we can see that there are no numbers that have only one ring around them, which would correspond to the tens place. This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> must in the tens place, which means that answer choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/d/56dc09b0de9fcf10f2fe99d2bae79f670af6c981.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(A) } 30105}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"94\" height=\"30\" /> is the answer.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal\">1999 CEMC Pascal</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Answer_Key\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 14\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_18\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 18\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 22 (page does not exist)\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 23 (page does not exist)\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 24 (page does not exist)\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1999 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 25 (page does not exist)\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=CEMC_Pascal\" title=\"CEMC Pascal\">CEMC Pascal</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table class=\"